hyperactive-interaction-in-the-words-of-the-human-superorganism-25 = unlines (cons "Truth, like history (*historia*, from *histōr*, the witness), has a witness too. *True* is Old English *trēowe*, faithful, firm, from the root that also gives *tree* and *trust*. Truth was first fidelity: the firmness of a word kept. The Greek gave the other half: *alētheia*, not-forgetting. So between them the two languages say that truth is a word kept firm and not forgotten. That is a content-addressed immutable store, described in Old English and Greek." nil)
